    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_019twTzk6nRiEPF8m92EQsJP",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F12",
                  "S1:F13",
                  "F41",
                  "F6",
                  "F4",
                  "S3:F16",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "S2:F34",
                  "S2:F15"
                ],
                "ref": "N1",
                "statement": "The UK is treating the US-brokered energy ceasefire as unproven: it backs the US peace effort while stepping up sanctions and winter air defence, on the expectation that Russian strikes on Ukraine's energy system will go on.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "F74",
                  "S16:F93",
                  "S16:F73",
                  "S16:F86",
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S10:F55",
                  "S14:F56"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "statement": "The new Prime Minister's first weeks show a deliberate pattern of moving the centre of government and its spending out of London: Number 10 North, the Great British Railways headquarters and train building in Derby, and an offer to devolve employment support to Scotland.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S17:F82",
                  "S16:F72",
                  "S16:F92",
                  "S16:F83"
                ],
                "ref": "N3",
                "statement": "The Scottish Government has so far cooperated in practice only on a small, specific scheme (matched £10m for defence colleges); whether the wider offer from the Prime Minister lands will first be tested at the National Economic Council meeting with the First Ministers.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F12",
                  "S1:F13",
                  "F41"
                ],
                "text": "The week's Ukraine news is best read against the energy ceasefire President Trump announced on 11 October. Ukraine said it would comply once a start date was confirmed, but by 13 October none had been set, and Russia was still bombarding Kyiv and its energy infrastructure."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F6",
                  "F4"
                ],
                "text": "London's response to the US-Russia agreement on 9 October had two parts: full support for the US-led peace effort, and continued pressure on Russia through sanctions."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S3:F16",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "S2:F34",
                  "S2:F40",
                  "S2:F15",
                  "S2:F33"
                ],
                "text": "The days that followed delivered on the pressure side. Sanctions now cover over 90% of Russia's oil producing capacity, a maritime services ban on Russian LNG is to be fully implemented, £45m of surface-to-air missiles are going to Ukraine with £10m of that sped up, and new winter funding was announced. Ministers have also been told to draw up further packages."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "Taken together, these steps suggest the UK is treating the ceasefire as unproven. It backs the US effort while preparing for a winter in which Russian strikes on energy go on."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F74",
                  "S16:F93",
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S10:F55",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "S16:F86"
                ],
                "text": "The domestic stories, which the daily Briefings carried separately under railways and Scotland, belong together. In his first weeks the new Prime Minister has set up Number 10 North and put Great British Railways' headquarters in Derby, with up to 450 staff. A £1bn northern train fleet is to be built at Alstom's Derby site, and he is offering to devolve employment support to Scotland."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "That looks like a deliberate plan to move the centre of government, and its spending, out of London, rather than a run of unrelated announcements."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S17:F82",
                  "S16:F83",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "text": "So far Holyrood has gone along with only one small, specific scheme: matching £10m for two defence technical colleges. The first real test of the wider offer is the National Economic Council's meeting with the First Ministers, due in a couple of weeks."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S8:F27",
                  "S8:F85"
                ],
                "text": "The next fixed date is the UK-EU summit in Brussels on 20 November, where the Prime Minister plans to seek a new food and farming deal."
              }
            ],
            "stories": [
              {
                "developments": [
                  "S16:F93",
                  "S16:F73",
                  "S16:F86",
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S10:F55",
                  "S14:F56"
                ],
                "title": "Moving government and investment out of London under the new Prime Minister"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
